    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0233" n="183" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv12/pg183"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 184.01502/22½: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Denmark</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grant-Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Commission to Negotiate Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-05-04">May 4, 1919</date>.<lb/>[Received May 5—9:50
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>354. Following from Libau: “Greene 19, May 3. Have carefully reviewed situation
                    in Finland with Gade who has prepared very able and exhaustive report on present
                    conditions Finland. We urge <pb facs="0234" n="184" xml:id="pg184"/> prompt
                    recognition of Finland as an independent country and financial aid from Allies
                    and America. Finland deserves recognition for her part in stopping westward
                    destructive flow of bolshevism and she has demonstrated her capacity for
                    self-government under difficult circumstances. Recognition and a loan now
                    essential to maintain her position. Her future should be determined quite apart
                    from problem of Baltic provinces. Greene.”<anchor n="30" xml:id="d59-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grant-Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d59-f1" n="30" xml:id="d59-f1-content">
                <p>The United States Government had already decided to recognize Finland; see
                    telegram No. 1992, May 5, 1919, 8 p.m., from the Commission to the Acting
                    Secretary of State, <ref target="frus1919Parisv02#pg_214"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1919, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 214</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
